From e5e027c3fcf638749a39438e4d4e7c9e93a994ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joseph Stackhouse <joseph.stackhouse@stack-tech.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 06:01:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] add a runtime research selector registry with activation
 reporting

Research selectors were plain environment variables read once at process start.
That had two consequences. A selector could not be toggled between requests on
an already-loaded model, so comparing two variants meant reloading. Worse, a
selector whose implementation was later removed became completely silent:
nothing reads an unknown environment variable, so nothing complains, and
same-package A/B testing cannot detect it either because both arms are missing
the same code.

This adds a small additive registry, one header and one source file using ggml
API visibility, that records every selector read and exposes the report. A selector that a deployment sets but that no surviving code reads
becomes detectable instead of invisible.

The registry changes no behaviour on its own. It is infrastructure the later
patches read through, and a caching accessor keeps hot paths off the lock.
